      "content": "I just double-checked the math on the simulation runs. You're right, it's more than a \"misalignment\"—the test they used is mathematically incompatible with the data they're claiming to have generated. If we publish this as a \"breakthrough\" and someone else replicates it only to find the statistical foundation is broken, it would be a disaster for the journal's reputation. \n\nBut looking at the alternative... if we reject this now, we lose the momentum for the special issue, and it's going to be a massive blow to our upcoming impact metrics. Is there any way we could frame this error as a \"methodological nuance\" or something similar in the discussion section so we don't lose the paper and the prestige?",
